    <glossary>
    <definition id="def-00001"><term>animism</term><meaning>belief that objects are alive and possess consciousness</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00002"><term>autobiographical memory</term><meaning>memories of our individual life experiences and events</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00003"><term>centration</term><meaning>tendency for preoperational children to focus on one characteristic of an object or situation at a time</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00004"><term>conservation</term><meaning>cognitive ability to understand that changing the appearance of a substance does not alter its mass, number, or volume</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00005"><term>egocentrism</term><meaning>preoperational children’s tendency to have limited logical reasoning abilities because they perceive things from only their own point of view, according to Piaget’s stage theory of cognitive development</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00006"><term>false belief</term><meaning>children’s belief that other people have the same knowledge they themselves possess</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00007"><term>operation</term><meaning>logical and reversible mental action</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00008"><term>preoperational stage</term><meaning>stage in which children begin to represent their thoughts and ideas using symbols such as words and images; the second stage in Piaget’s stage theory of cognitive development</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00009"><term>scaffolding</term><meaning>process of providing temporary learning support when tasks are just outside a person’s range of ability</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00010"><term>sociocultural theory of cognitive development</term><meaning>theory of development proposed by Vygotsky that emphasizes the role of social and cultural factors on individual cognitive development</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00011"><term>symbolic representation</term><meaning>representation of thoughts and ideas using symbols, such as words and images</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00012"><term>theory of mind</term><meaning>awareness that our beliefs and perspectives are different from other people’s</meaning></definition>
    <definition id="def-00013"><term>zone of proximal development (ZPD)</term><meaning>range of activities that cannot be completed alone but can be accomplished with the aid of a more skilled adult or peer</meaning></definition>
    </glossary>
</document>